# Week 1 Research Journal Summary

During the first week of my AR/VR research journey, I focused on building a strong understanding of the core technologies behind Extended Reality (XR).
Rather than jumping directly into development, I studied the fundamental concepts that enable modern VR and AR systems to function effectively.

Throughout the week, I explored the different types of immersive technologies (XR, AR, VR, and MR), learned how VR headsets track user movement using
inside-out and outside-in tracking systems, and understood the importance of six degrees of freedom (6DOF) for creating realistic experiences. I also studied the hardware inside VR headsets, including sensors such as gyroscopes, accelerometers, cameras, and IMUs, and learned how these components work together to provide accurate motion tracking.

In addition, I researched common VR challenges such as motion sickness and latency, gaining an understanding of how system performance directly affects
user comfort. The week concluded with an introduction to Spatial Computing, where I learned how digital objects can understand, interact with, and remain
anchored to the physical environment.

The topic that interested me the most this week was Spatial Computing because it showed me that XR extends far beyond gaming. Learning how digital content
can seamlessly integrate with the physical world gave me a broader perspective on the potential of immersive technologies in fields such as education,
healthcare, architecture, manufacturing, and many other industries.

Overall, this week established a solid theoretical foundation for future research. The knowledge gained will support upcoming topics such as OpenXR, SLAM,
hand tracking, eye tracking, and Unreal Engine XR development, ultimately preparing me to build immersive spatial computing applications.
